1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,000
So here we are, We're out in the woods, We're getting ready to take a hike.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And I talked to the senior patrol leader. I say,

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Listen, have you reviewed hike safety and everything with everybody?

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Yes, Yes, sir, I have. He says:

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Have you talked about the buddy? Yes, We've talked about the buddy system.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,500
I said: What are you going to do if you discover one of the boys has gotten separated from the group?

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,000
And he said: Well, you know, we will stop and we'll retrace our stops,

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,500
We'll kind of fan out, We'll be able to find them.

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,000
So what are you going to do if one of the adults gets separated from the group?

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
He says: Well, that really depends on who it is.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Smart boy, that senior patrol leader.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
This is podcast number 93.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Oh

15
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:17,000
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast. This is Clark Green.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,500
Let's take a look and see who's been in touch over the past week.

17
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,500
We heard from Mike Manzer via Facebook.

18
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:26,500
He said I enjoyed the panel discussion in podcast number 92.

19
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:28,000
And I hope that you do it often.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,500
The discussion about the new leader training modules was especially timely.

21
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,500
I've used the others that were discussed and found that each one needed some improvement.

22
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,500
I look forward to reading through the new youth leadership training module

23
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:41,500
and hopefully adopting it with success.

24
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:43,500
Mike, I'm glad you enjoyed the discussion.

25
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:45,000
I'm glad it was useful and timely.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,500
One of the things we did discuss was a new troop leadership training syllabus

27
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,000
that the BSA has recently published.

28
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
We are going to have Scoutmaster panel discussions on a regular basis-

29
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
I would say about once a month- on the podcast here

30
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And you can participate in those by getting in touch

31
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,500
and sending me a question or a comment or a topic suggestion

32
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:13,000
And your question may be selected to participate during the recording of that podcast via telephone.

33
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
We'll call you up and you can ask your question

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,500
and you can have a little exchange with the Scoutmaster panel.

35
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,500
We did that last time and I think we found that pretty useful

36
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,000
and we really enjoyed doing it again.

37
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So you can be in touch by emailing me at scoutmastercg at Verizonnet

38
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,500
and you can also get in touch through the contact form at scoutmastercgcom.

39
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,500
I also heard from my friend Scout of Richard up in Canada.

40
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:45,500
Richard and I exchanged some ideas and some advice via email back in the spring

41
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,500
about moving his troop towards more youth leadership.

42
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,500
He sent me a progress report last week,

43
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:54,500
He said shortly after the discussion we had.

44
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:59,500
I took the advice you provided me back to my fellow adult leaders

45
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:01,500
and we laid out an approach I thought we should take.

46
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,500
It was generally well received by them,

47
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:07,500
although I do recall there were a few moments of convincing and explanation,

48
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,500
But in the end we all got on the same page.

49
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,500
Then we took these things and shared them with our youth leaders

50
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:14,500
And then, if we fast forward a few months,

51
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:18,500
all the adults have attempted to be as consistent as possible

52
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,500
with support and advice when we are asked by the youth.

53
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,500
And although we're moving a little bit slowly with plans-

54
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,500
and that might be an adult perspective, I agree, Richard-

55
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,500
Most of the time it's an adult perspective.

56
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,500
Scouts tend to move a little slowly with planning.

57
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,500
I do think that it is gradually moving forward

58
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:38,500
towards the youth leadership goal we laid out in the spring.

59
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,500
There doesn't seem to be any real shortcuts here at all.

60
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,500
It just has to happen when it happens.

61
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,500
Your continued support and advice is always appreciated

62
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,500
And I sincerely thank you for your wealth of resources

63
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:52,500
through the blog and the podcast.

64
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,500
Well, Richard, I'm really happy to hear from you.

65
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,500
I'm glad that the advice that we shared with you

66
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,500
back in the spring is now bearing some fruit,

67
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,500
And your email reminds me that scouting is really a process.

68
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:05,500
It's a process where we're always traveling,

69
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,500
We're always moving forward,

70
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:09,500
but we're never really reaching a goal.

71
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,500
It's a landmark that we're going to get to

72
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,500
and we're going to say, okay, hey, we're done,

73
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,500
We've accomplished everything that we needed to accomplish.

74
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:19,500
Nope, because scouts are different,

75
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:22,500
The way that they look at the world is different,

76
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,500
but the scouting program and the genius behind it

77
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,500
meets them where they are and lets them learn skills

78
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:31,500
to become good, productive, happy people

79
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:34,500
and to contribute to their communities and the world.

80
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,500
And we're going to talk a little bit more about that

81
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:39,500
in Scout Master Ship in seven minutes or less.

82
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,500
And then we do have an email question to answer

83
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:44,500
and that'll take care of this week's podcast.

84
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:46,500
So let's get started, shall we?

85
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:41,500
In seven minutes or less.

86
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,500
You know, one of my all-time favorite scouts online

87
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,500
is Andy over at Ask Andy,

88
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:56,500
And if you haven't visited there,

89
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:01,500
you go to the United States Scouting Service Project website,

90
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,500
which, if you're listening to this,

91
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:05,500
you've probably already been there.

92
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,500
So go over and look at Ask Andy on that website.

93
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,500
It's a kind of an advice column for scouts

94
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:14,500
and it is just great.

95
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:17,500
And I saw something in answer to a question

96
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,500
in his latest column and here's an excerpt from it.

97
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,500
I wanted to include it here

98
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:24,500
because I think this has said something

99
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:27,500
that I kind of try and say all the time,

100
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:30,500
but Andy has said it so well.

101
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:32,500
I don't really think I can improve on it

102
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:35,500
As this: our charge as volunteers in the movement

103
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:37,500
is to help create tomorrow's productive,

104
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:39,500
responsible, happy citizens.

105
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,500
We can't do that if we don't keep our doors in our arms

106
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,500
open to these young men,

107
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,500
understanding that the ultimate volunteer isn't us,

108
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:49,500
it's the scout himself.

109
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:51,500
If he gets sick and tired of our nagging him

110
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:54,500
instead of positive reinforcement of good habits,

111
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,500
or dinging him when it comes to rank advancement,

112
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,500
even when he's proven he can knock off

113
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:02,500
the skill set required without breaking his sweat,

114
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:04,500
then he's going to walk,

115
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:07,500
And that's the very last thing we want to have happen,

116
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:10,500
because then we can no longer instill in him

117
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:12,500
the ideals and values of the movement.

118
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:15,500
Yes, we're a movement and not an activity.

119
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,500
So here's my single guideline:

120
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:21,500
Look only at the individual scout,

121
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,500
See only the individual,

122
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,500
Understand only the individual,

123
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:29,500
Each, one by one and one at a time.

124
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:32,500
Each and every young man who crosses the doorstep

125
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,500
of scouting is unique

126
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,500
and he lives in a world and an environment

127
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:38,500
unique to him and him alone.

128
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:40,500
There is no other like him in the world

129
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:43,500
than we dare not apply an arbitrary measure

130
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:46,500
in this one particular dimension that we call active.

131
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:50,500
Now, obviously, Andy was answering a very specific question

132
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,500
about, you know, this age-old question

133
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:56,500
of how do we determine if a scout is active or not.

134
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:59,500
But I think in that statement he has encapsulated

135
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:03,500
the most productive, the happiest

136
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:08,500
and the most stress-free approach to scouting,

137
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:14,500
And that is that scouting is a highly individualized thing

138
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:17,500
And as scout leaders it is our job

139
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:20,500
to make sure that the scouting program

140
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,500
affects that individual scout

141
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,500
And that's it. That is it.

142
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:30,500
Everything else is for the birds.

143
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:36,500
One of the fellows who runs the US Scouting Service project.

144
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:39,500
he says if it's not for the boys, it's for the birds,

145
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:41,500
And I absolutely agree.

146
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,500
When we're focused on the individual needs

147
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:45,500
of each individual scout,

148
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,500
everything begins to fall into place.

149
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,500
If you want to back that up even more,

150
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,500
go over to PTC Media

151
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:56,500
and listen to the last SMM podcast by Scout Master Jerry,

152
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:58,500
in which he recorded a session. he did

153
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:02,500
a training session, he did about Scout Master Conferences,

154
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:07,500
And Jerry talks about understanding the needs

155
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:11,500
the personality and the way that scouting impacts

156
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,500
an individual scout,

157
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,500
And he does it very, very well.

158
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,500
I encourage you to go over and listen to that.

159
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,500
I can't present a program that impacts an individual scout,

160
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,500
wherever he is, however he comes to us,

161
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:25,500
whatever his environment is,

162
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:28,500
whatever he has to deal with outside of scouting,

163
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:30,500
then we're failing what I consider to be

164
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:34,500
the most central object of our work as scout leaders.

165
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:36,500
If you're looking for the unified theory of scouting,

166
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:37,500
that's got to be it

167
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:39,500
And I really appreciate it.

168
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:42,500
I appreciate Andy writing as he does

169
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:44,500
and that particular statement that I just read,

170
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,500
And I appreciate Scout Master Jerry over at the SMM podcast

171
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:51,500
putting that so clearly in the recent podcast

172
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,500
he did about Scout Master Conferences.

173
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:55,500
I encourage you to look at both of them

174
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:58,500
and I sincerely hope that it all helps you refocus

175
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:01,500
your efforts on those individual scouts.

176
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:23,500
Hi, this is Dr Paul Auerbach

177
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:25,500
and you are listening to Clark Green

178
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:27,500
on the Scout Master podcast.

179
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:33,500
Find me a letter, send it by name

180
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:35,500
Email. that is folks.

181
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:39,500
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

182
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,500
Well, I got this question from Mike Beck,

183
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:51,500
who is a Scout Master from Troop 61 in Brandon, Florida.

184
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,500
I have a scout who is not on the Patrol Leaders Council

185
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
but he's interested in performing a leadership project

186
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
in order to make Star.

187
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:00,500
Mike's referring to a common statement that you'll find

188
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,500
in the Star or Life rank requirements

189
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:06,500
that talks about a Scout Master assigned leadership project

190
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:08,500
to help the troop.

191
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,500
Mike goes on to say: do you have any suggestions?

192
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,500
Whatever it is, I'd like it to be at least two things.

193
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,500
I'd like it to require regular activity,

194
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,500
so it's something that wouldn't be just completed in two weeks

195
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:20,500
around a single camp out,

196
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:22,500
and that it would cause the scout to consciously think

197
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:24,500
about serving the needs of others in his troop.

198
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:26,500
So I got back in touch with Mike

199
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:30,500
and I would start the process of identifying

200
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:34,500
what that Scout Master assigned leadership project

201
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:36,500
is going to look like by sitting down with a scout

202
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,500
and asking him what ideas he has.

203
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,500
You know how about the Senior Patrol Leader?

204
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:44,500
Is the Senior Patrol Leader need somebody to do a specific thing,

205
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:46,500
Not necessarily just like a limited task,

206
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:48,500
but does he need somebody to occupy a role

207
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:50,500
or a function for a period of time

208
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:53,500
that's going to help him with his job in the troop?

209
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:55,500
Scout Master assigned leadership projects

210
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:58,500
are very loosely defined, and necessarily so,

211
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:01,500
because what it does is. it gives you the opportunity

212
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:05,500
as a Scout Master and the scout the opportunity as a leader

213
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:08,500
to find different ways- that might be slightly outside

214
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:11,500
the definitions of positions of responsibility in scouting-

215
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:13,500
to help his fellow Scouts.

216
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,500
I don't think that this has to be one thing.

217
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,500
It could be several things.

218
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:19,500
What's important is that you and the Scout agree

219
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,500
on a set of expectations.

220
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:23,500
I have a Scout right now who was a Patrol Leader.

221
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:24,500
He's a Star Scout.

222
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:26,500
Most of the positions of responsibility

223
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:28,500
within the Patrol Leaders Council

224
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:31,500
that aren't directly patrol related are occupied.

225
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,500
I have an instructor,

226
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:35,500
We have a Senior Patrol Leader,

227
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:37,500
Assistant Senior Patrol Leader, Scribe and Quartermaster.

228
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:39,500
This Scout came to me and said:

229
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:41,500
what leadership position can I have?

230
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,500
He and I talked for a while

231
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,500
and I described what in basketball

232
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:47,500
I think is called a Pivot Man.

233
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,500
I'm not a big basketball expert,

234
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:51,500
but I used it just to illustrate.

235
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:53,500
you know, a Pivot Man in basketball

236
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:55,500
is somebody who can play just about any position,

237
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,500
can get in there and help the team out

238
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,500
whenever he's needed.

239
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,500
I said: you know, you can be our Pivot Man

240
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,500
in the troop.

241
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:05,500
There'll be plenty of opportunities for you

242
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:07,500
to step in and to help out

243
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:09,500
in a leadership capacity

244
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,500
with many different things

245
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,500
And you and I'll just keep an eye on it

246
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:15,500
And our expectations will be

247
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,500
that when we're ready to evaluate

248
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:19,500
how well you have done

249
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:21,500
that, we will have had an ongoing discussion

250
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:23,500
about the different tasks that you've undertaken.

251
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:25,500
So you need to be conscious

252
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:27,500
whenever you do

253
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:29,500
something to help the troop out.

254
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:31,500
Just kind of keep it in mind.

255
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:33,500
You might even write it down.

256
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:35,500
It's a scout-master conference.

257
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:37,500
That's what we'll be talking about

258
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:39,500
And I'm going to have an ongoing dialogue

259
